{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nThe `tickets` pack grows ~2.0 KiB/commit at ~14K commits/day because each enrichment step\n(ENQUEUE/CLAIM/DONE_ENRICH) and each `TICKET_DIGEST` is written as its OWN commit, re-emitting the\n270 KB root tree every time. Below-horizon collapse (S1) removes the historical churn; W1 removes\nthe churn AT THE SOURCE so it does not re-accumulate, which is what keeps the pack under budget\nafter the reclaim lands.\n\n## Scope\n\nBatch the derived enrichment quartet + digest for a single logical mutation into ONE write/commit\n(one root-tree re-emission) instead of 3-4, without changing observable ticket state, event\nsemantics, signatures, or optimistic-concurrency guarantees. Preserve per-event signing/verifiability.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nThe history reclaim of already-accumulated commits (that is S1). No change to the event schema's\nobservable contract beyond commit batching.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A single logical mutation that today emits the enrichment quartet + digest as separate commits emits ONE commit (one root-tree re-emission), measured on a fixture.\n- [ ] Observable reduced ticket state is unchanged vs the pre-batch behavior (reducer-equality fixture passes).\n- [ ] Each batched event retains its individual signature and passes `rebar verify-authorship`.\n- [ ] Optimistic-concurrency / audit guarantees are preserved (existing concurrency tests pass).\n- [ ] The measured commits-per-mutation and per-mutation root-tree re-emission count drop as designed.\n\n## Portability\n\nPure library/store change; no dependence on any single CI provider.\n\n## Implementation plan (2026-08-31, revised after BLOCK review)\n\n### Worked example / exact quartet\n\nThe one-commit fixture is the self-heal path for a ticket whose digest is `present-stale` but has no live queue entry. Today one drain emits, as four separate ticket-store commits, these reducer-ignored sidecars for the same ticket: `ENQUEUE_ENRICH {ticket_id, not_before_ns}`, `CLAIM_ENRICH {ticket_id, drainer_id, lease_expires_ns}`, `TICKET_DIGEST {schema, digest, content_hash, model, digest_hash_version}`, and `DONE_ENRICH {ticket_id}`. W1 will make that logical enrichment mutation produce those same four event files in one git commit. For an already-enqueued ticket, the prior durable `ENQUEUE_ENRICH` remains the existing input event; the drain write lane batches the derived `CLAIM_ENRICH` + `TICKET_DIGEST` + `DONE_ENRICH` into one commit.\n\n### Mechanism\n\nUse the existing event batching primitives; do not add or change event types. `_commands._seam.batch_sink()` still composes every event through the normal HLC/UUID/attribution/secret-screen/authorship-signing seam. `_store.event_append.batch_write_and_push()` already commits all buffered event files atomically under one write lock and one push.\n\n1. Add an enrichment-local reservation helper in `rebar.llm.enrich_drain` backed by non-git internal state under the tracker git-dir. During the collect phase, while holding the existing drain advisory lock, reserve selected tickets with `{ticket_id, drainer_id, lease_expires_ns}`. Other drainers consult this reservation set before selecting candidates. Expired reservations are ignored/reclaimed. This preserves duplicate-spend prevention without needing a visible `CLAIM_ENRICH` commit before the LLM call.\n2. For each successful item, finalize through one `_seam.batch_sink` and one `batch_write_and_push`: emit a self-heal `ENQUEUE_ENRICH` first when collect selected a stale unqueued ticket, then emit `CLAIM_ENRICH`, then `TICKET_DIGEST`, then `DONE_ENRICH`. Existing public readers still see the same ordered event stream after commit; only the number of git commits/root-tree emissions changes.\n3. For already-enqueued items, preserve the pre-existing `ENQUEUE_ENRICH` as the input event and batch `CLAIM_ENRICH` + `TICKET_DIGEST` + `DONE_ENRICH` into one finalization commit. This is the maximum reduction compatible with an enqueue that was already durable before W1's drain starts.\n4. Failure semantics: if enrich fails transiently, leave the reservation until its lease expires and do not write `DONE_ENRICH`, matching the old retry-after-lease posture without committing a stranded CLAIM. If enrich is a permanent input rejection, batch `CLAIM_ENRICH` + `DONE_ENRICH` (and self-heal ENQUEUE when applicable) so it is terminal exactly as before. If the batch commit fails, the existing batch primitive rolls the whole write back, so no partial derived event is staged/on disk; the reservation expires for retry.\n5. Keep public `queue.enqueue`, `queue.claim`, `queue.mark_done`, and `digest_sidecar.emit` backward-compatible. The new reservation/batch path is private to `enrich_drain.drain` and uses the same reducers/readers after events commit.\n\n### Alternatives considered\n\nA. Batch only `TICKET_DIGEST + DONE_ENRICH`: rejected by plan-review and AC1 because the claim/digest/done lane still emits multiple commits. B. Defer all events with no reservation: rejected because concurrent drainers could duplicate LLM calls. C. Hold the advisory drain lock across the LLM to prevent duplicates: rejected because existing concurrency work explicitly requires the lock to be released before LLM calls. Chosen option: internal leased reservations plus one final batched event commit; it preserves concurrency while changing only commit batching.\n\n### Test plan / RED-first oracle\n\n- Add a fixture test in `tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py` for the self-heal stale-digest path: create a stale digest, run one drain, assert exactly one new commit and one new root-tree emission contains `ENQUEUE_ENRICH`, `CLAIM_ENRICH`, `TICKET_DIGEST`, and `DONE_ENRICH` for that ticket.\n- Add a fixture test for an already-enqueued item: enqueue first, then drain, assert the drain adds one commit containing `CLAIM_ENRICH`, `TICKET_DIGEST`, and `DONE_ENRICH` and public postconditions match the old behavior (`freshness == present-fresh`, queue `done` true, `pending` false).\n- Add/keep `rebar verify-authorship` on the fixture after batched writes to prove each batched event carries individual authorship material.\n- Run existing concurrency coverage (`test_concurrent_drains_make_zero_duplicate_llm_calls`, stale writeback/re-enqueue, lease/permanent-failure tests) so optimistic duplicate-spend and audit behavior stay covered by the shared drain suite.\n- Preflight with targeted pytest first, then `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `make test` before push.\n\n### Surface-parity census\n\nCLI command: in scope through `rebar enrich --drain`/`cmd_enrich` because it routes to `enrich_drain.drain`. MCP/API: no separate schema change; enrichment callers share the same library drain core, accepted residual gap unless a direct MCP enrich surface appears. Library facade: in scope through direct `enrich_drain.drain`, `queue`, and `digest_sidecar` fixture tests. Automated callers: in scope because `_store.event_append.write_and_push -> _maybe_enrich_drain -> maybe_drain -> drain` remains the only automated path. Read/dual direction: in scope through `latest_ticket_digest`, `freshness`, `queue.reduce_ticket`, and `pending_enrichment`; reducer-visible ticket state stays unchanged.\n\n### Acceptance mapping\n\nAC1/AC5: commit-count/root-tree tests. AC2: public reducer/reader postconditions. AC3: `verify-authorship`. AC4: unchanged public queue contracts plus existing/revised drain concurrency tests.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788238541150166001,"uuid":"c6b4294f-0f11-42fb-a289-8f636b503d17"}